1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,200
Applied Resonance starts with a simple idea:

2
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:17,500
Before you can notice that a machine sounds wrong,
you have to learn what normal sounds like.

3
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:23,500
Here, the real G2 microphone listens to a normal pump
for thirty seconds and builds a baseline.

4
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,000
The system stays in LISTENING.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,500
When playback changes to an abnormal recording from
the same pump at the same volume, the change persists...

6
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,000
and an alert appears on the glasses.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,500
I tap the temple once, and it saves the previous
ten seconds as evidence.

8
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:47,500
When the normal sound returns,
it goes back to LISTENING.

9
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,500
That recovery matters because
this is not just a one-way tripwire.

10
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:55,500
I also wanted to know whether the detector actually worked
beyond a good-looking demo.

11
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:04,000
I tested it on 9,755 industrial recordings from DCASE,
a standard benchmark for unusual machine sounds.

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:18,500
It reached an AUC of 0.693 on fans and 0.880 on pumps,
above the published DCASE2020 reference baselines.

13
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:24,000
Six months ago, I had never built for wearable hardware.

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Now I have a working loop from glasses, to phone,
to model, and back to the display.

15
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,500
That is what I am proudest of.

16
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:39,000
I followed one question from my boxing gym
all the way to a real prototype.
